  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "Let \\(v,\\) \\(w,\\) \\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) and \\(z\\) be the degree measures of the five angles of a pentagon. Suppose \\(v\\lt w\\lt x\\lt y\\lt z\\) and \\(v,\\) \\(w,\\) \\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) \\(z\\) form an arithmetic sequence. Find the value of \\(x.\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(72\\)",
    "B": "\\(84\\)",
    "C": "\\(90\\)",
    "D": "\\(108\\)",
    "E": "\\(120\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The common difference need not be found, because terms equally far from the middle have opposite deviations from it. Let \\(d>0\\) be the common difference. Then\n\\[\nv=x-2d,\\quad w=x-d,\\quad y=x+d,\\quad z=x+2d,\n\\]\nso \\(v+z=2x\\) and \\(w+y=2x\\).\n\nA pentagon's interior angles sum to \\((5-2)\\cdot180=540\\) degrees. Consequently\n\\[\n(v+z)+(w+y)+x=5x=540.\n\\]\nThus \\(x=\\boxed{108}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
